WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.250
Almost every part has holes: for bolts, pins or oil.

2
00:00:05.799 --> 00:00:08.109
The standard tool is the twist drill.

3
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:12.991
On a flat block it slides a fraction sideways before it bites.

4
00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.328
That is called drill wander.

5
00:00:16.228 --> 00:00:21.815
So first you make a dimple with a centre drill: short and stiff, with a broad point.

6
00:00:22.365 --> 00:00:26.138
In it, the twist drill finds its place straight away.

7
00:00:27.038 --> 00:00:32.741
Then the twist drill: two cutting edges at the point, and two grooves in a spiral.

8
00:00:33.291 --> 00:00:36.252
Along each flute the chips turn their way out.

9
00:00:36.802 --> 00:00:38.044
In mild steel,

10
00:00:38.244 --> 00:00:43.631
an 8 millimetre high-speed steel drill runs at about 25 metres a minute.

11
00:00:44.181 --> 00:00:47.838
That is 995 revolutions a minute.

12
00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:50.362
The point is a cone.

13
00:00:50.912 --> 00:00:57.402
Its angle is the point angle: 118 degrees on ordinary high-speed steel drills.

14
00:00:57.952 --> 00:00:59.786
That point centres the drill.

15
00:01:00.686 --> 00:01:05.507
This hole does not go through: a blind hole, with a cone-shaped bottom.

16
00:01:06.057 --> 00:01:09.389
20 millimetres deep takes about 8 seconds.

17
00:01:10.589 --> 00:01:13.143
A drilled hole is a few hundredths big.

18
00:01:13.693 --> 00:01:17.248
If it must be tighter, a second tool follows.

19
00:01:17.798 --> 00:01:25.301
For 10 millimetres in H7 you first drill 9.8, and a reamer takes off the last 0.2.

20
00:01:26.201 --> 00:01:29.324
The H says the hole may never be smaller than 10.

21
00:01:29.874 --> 00:01:32.324
The 7 says how wide the band is.

22
00:01:32.874 --> 00:01:39.573
So the hole measures between 10.000 and 10.015 millimetres.

23
00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:43.085
Hole and pin together are called the fit.

24
00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:46.410
This pin slides in: a clearance fit.

25
00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:52.533
For a thread you first drill the tap drill hole: the screw size minus the pitch.

26
00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:59.285
For M8, with a pitch of 1.25 millimetres, that is a 6.8 drill.

27
00:02:00.185 --> 00:02:02.461
Then the tap cuts the thread: tapping.

28
00:02:03.011 --> 00:02:08.600
Taps break easily, above all in a blind hole, where the chips cannot get away.

29
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.763
A large fitted hole, such as 40 H7 for a bearing,

30
00:02:13.963 --> 00:02:16.598
is opened up by a boring head: boring.

31
00:02:17.149 --> 00:02:22.025
The band here is 0 to plus 0.025 millimetres.

32
00:02:23.225 --> 00:02:28.011
In deep holes, what counts is the depth divided by the diameter.

33
00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:33.742
Up to about 3 to 5 times, a twist drill goes through in one go.

34
00:02:34.292 --> 00:02:39.148
This hole, 6 millimetres and 90 deep, is 15 times.

35
00:02:39.697 --> 00:02:44.100
Then the flutes fill up, and it is all about chip evacuation.

36
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.808
In peck drilling the drill keeps pulling back to clear the chips.

37
00:02:49.358 --> 00:02:54.815
54 seconds of cutting plus 30 seconds of pulling back: 84 seconds a hole.

38
00:02:55.715 --> 00:02:59.967
With through-spindle coolant, coolant sprays through the drill itself,

39
00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:01.328
at high pressure.

40
00:03:01.878 --> 00:03:03.852
That flushes the chips out.

41
00:03:04.402 --> 00:03:08.814
With a carbide drill and no pecking: 13.5 seconds.

42
00:03:09.363 --> 00:03:14.788
At 10,000 parts a year, that saves 196 hours.

43
00:03:15.988 --> 00:03:18.322
Deeper still, or extremely straight?

44
00:03:18.872 --> 00:03:22.216
Then a gun drill: one cutting edge and a hollow shank.

45
00:03:23.116 --> 00:03:28.274
The coolant runs through the shank at high pressure, and carries the chips back out,

46
00:03:28.473 --> 00:03:30.516
along a groove on the outside.

47
00:03:31.716 --> 00:03:35.652
A hole can have the right size and still be in the wrong place.

48
00:03:36.202 --> 00:03:38.814
That is what the position tolerance is for.

49
00:03:39.364 --> 00:03:44.020
The hole’s axis must lie within a circle of 0.1 millimetres.

50
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:50.052
That circle sits round the spot set by the theoretically exact dimensions.

51
00:03:50.602 --> 00:03:55.248
Those are sizes in a small box, with no tolerance of their own.

52
00:03:56.148 --> 00:04:04.986
The measuring machine measures hole 3: 0.024 millimetres off in X, and 0.032 in Y.

53
00:04:05.536 --> 00:04:08.996
That is 0.04 from the exact spot.

54
00:04:09.546 --> 00:04:15.655
The tolerance is a diameter, so the position is 2 times that: 0.08.

55
00:04:16.855 --> 00:04:20.222
Perpendicularity says whether the hole stands straight.

56
00:04:20.772 --> 00:04:26.754
Its axis must be square to face A, within 0.05 millimetres.

57
00:04:27.654 --> 00:04:31.125
In series the part goes onto a functional gauge.

58
00:04:31.675 --> 00:04:35.019
That is a plate with pins at the exact places.

59
00:04:35.919 --> 00:04:38.963
If the part fits, the position is good.

60
00:04:39.513 --> 00:04:41.440
Quick, and hard to get wrong.
